{
  "id": "detect-cycle-directed-graph",
  "questionId": "detect-cycle-directed-graph",
  "slug": "detect-cycle-directed-graph",
  "title": "Detect Cycle in a Directed Graph",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Graphs",
  "patterns": [
    "graph dfs/bfs",
    "cycle detection",
    "directed graph"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "a cycle means you can follow directed edges and return to a node you're still in the middle of visiting",
    "plain visited/unvisited tracking isn't enough, since two different paths can both legitimately reach the same finished node without that being a cycle",
    "track three states per node: unvisited, currently on the active DFS path (in progress), and fully finished",
    "if DFS ever follows an edge into a node that's currently on the active path, that's a cycle; when a node's DFS call returns, move it from 'in progress' to 'finished'"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a single visited set, which incorrectly flags a DAG as cyclic when two paths converge on the same finished node",
    "not handling self loops (an edge from a node directly to itself), which are trivially cycles",
    "only checking from node 0 instead of starting a fresh DFS from every unvisited node, missing cycles in disconnected components"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a directed graph with `n` nodes labeled 0 to n-1, described by\na list of directed `edges` where each edge is [u, v] meaning there's an\nedge from u to v.\n\n**Return true if the graph contains a cycle, a path that starts and ends at the same node by following edges in their direction, and false otherwise.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 100000",
      "edges are directed, self loops are possible"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "graph with no edges at all",
      "a self loop (edge from a node to itself)",
      "a disconnected graph where only one component has a cycle",
      "a graph where an undirected view would look cyclic but the directed edges don't form a directed cycle"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 4, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[2,3]]",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "the edges form a simple chain with no way back to an earlier node"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 3, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[2,0]]",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 0 is a cycle"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 4, edges = [[0,1],[0,2],[1,3],[2,3]]",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "multiple paths reach node 3, but none of them loop back, this is a DAG"
      }
    ],
